    <region left="0" top="1395" width="326" height="23" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Using original TIP41, TIP42 and 1N914 components.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="1998" width="777" height="38" color="#000000" bgColor="#ffff80" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="777" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="background-color: #ffff80;">3. Select the amplifier to be used (provide a justification based on the parameters discussed in class) and include the model in LTSpice.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2034" width="772" height="166" color="#000000">
      <picture>
        <raw format="png" encoding="base64"></raw>
      </picture>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2196" width="522" height="221" color="#000000">
      <picture>
        <raw format="png" encoding="base64"></raw>
      </picture>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2439" width="789" height="323" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="776" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>
            <span style="font-weight: bold;">I have asked chatGPT to provide a part number that can replace the original 741 and provide a justification for the decision:</span>
            <br />"I selected the OPA134 for both stages of the audio amplifier because it operates from the existing ±12 V supply and offers more bandwidth, a higher slew rate, and lower input voltage noise than the 741<br />- Bandwidth: Its 8 MHz gain-bandwidth product, compared with 1 MHz for the LM741, gives the feedback loop more gain near 20 kHz. This helps the circuit maintain its intended gain across the audio band.<br />- Slew rate: Its 20 V/µs slew rate, compared with 0.5 V/µs for the LM741, gives it more margin to follow large, high-frequency signals without slew-rate distortion.<br />- Input noise and bias current: Its voltage noise is 8 nV/√Hz at 1 kHz, compared with 30 nV/√Hz for the LM741. Its low input bias current is especially useful in U1 because it reduces the DC voltage error developed across the 1 MΩ input resistor. www.ti.com<br />In this design, the OPA134 buffers the input signal in U1 and controls the output stage through feedback in U2. The TIP41C and TIP42C supply the current required by the 8 Ω speaker."<br /><br /><span style="font-weight: bold;">I have also shared both plots (OPA134 and 745) and asked if the justifications above can be observed in them:</span><br />"Yes. The plot shows nearly constant small-signal gain and phase across the audio band. The solid green trace falls from near 0 dB to about −180 mdB (−0.18 dB) at 20 kHz. That’s about 98% of the input amplitude. The dotted green trace stays close to −180°, so the output remains inverted.<br /><br />Compared with your earlier LM741 plot, the OPA134 circuit stays closer to −180° at 20 kHz. That is consistent with the higher gain-bandwidth product."<br /></p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
  </regions>
